answer:
**Parameters.** `total = 100`, `pageSize = 5`, so `totalPages = ceil(total / pageSize) = 20`. Pages are numbered from 1 (state this convention).

**Core formulas** (object index `i` is zero-based, 0 to 99)
- Page of an object: `page = floor(i / pageSize) + 1`. Object 0 to 4 are on page 1, object 5 to 9 on page 2, and object 99 is on page 20. For a one-based object position `n`, use `ceil(n / pageSize)`.
- Items on page `p`: from `start = (p - 1) * pageSize` up to `end = min(start + pageSize, total)` (exclusive), a slice of the list.
- Previous exists if `p > 1`; Next exists if `p < totalPages`.

**Class design**
```
class Paginator<T> {
    private final List<T> items; private final int pageSize;
    Paginator(List<T> items, int pageSize) {...}
    int totalPages()      { return (items.size() + pageSize - 1) / pageSize; }
    List<T> getPage(int p){ validate(p); int s=(p-1)*pageSize;
                            return items.subList(s, Math.min(s+pageSize, items.size())); }
    int pageOf(int index) { return index / pageSize + 1; }
    boolean hasPrev(int p){ return p > 1; }
    boolean hasNext(int p){ return p < totalPages(); }
}
```
The UI keeps only `currentPage`. It renders `getPage(currentPage)`, page links `1..totalPages`, and Previous and Next buttons that are disabled when `hasPrev` or `hasNext` is false. Clicking a link just sets `currentPage`.

**Edge cases.** Empty list (zero pages: show a message), last page shorter than the page size when `total` is not a multiple of 5, page numbers out of range (clamp or reject), and a very large number of pages (show a window such as `1 ... 8 9 [10] 11 12 ... 20` instead of all links).

**Scaling.** For large data, paginate on the server with `LIMIT 5 OFFSET (p-1)*5` and a total count query. Offset gets slow for deep pages, so use keyset pagination (`WHERE id > lastSeenId ORDER BY id LIMIT 5`) when users only go next or previous.
